module

public import FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«602»

/-!
# Erdős Problem 602: almost disjoint countable sets with no singleton intersections have Property B

Generated by an AI agent and machine-checked; not reviewed by a human expert.

## Statement

`main` has exactly the type of the upstream theorem `Erdos602.erdos_602` (Formal Conjectures,
`FormalConjectures/ErdosProblems/602.lean`, commit 9097e2f9). Let `(A i)`, `i ∈ I`, be a family of
countably infinite subsets of `α` such that, for `i ≠ j`, the set `A i ∩ A j` is finite and does not
have exactly one element. Then some `f : α → Fin 2` is non-constant on every `A i`. The types `α`
and `I` are arbitrary, in independent universes, and nothing beyond choice is assumed (no CH, no
MA). The axioms used are `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`. The proof only uses that
each `A i` is countable with at least two points; `exists_colouring` is the theorem in this form.

This is an affirmative answer to the headline problem. No defect is known in the formal statement;
it matches the source (Erdős 1987, p. 227). Only a remark in the upstream docstring is inaccurate:
an uncountable family cannot be moved to `ℕ` by bijections of the single sets.

## Outline of the proof

All colourings are partial. A set is *split* if it contains both colours, a point is *settled* if
all sets through it are split, a colouring is *closed* if all coloured points are settled. By Zorn's
lemma there is a maximal closed colouring, and it is enough to extend a closed colouring that leaves
some `A a` unsplit. This is done in countably many steps, and the intermediate colourings satisfy:
every coloured point `z` has a *guard*, a finite set of indices of sets that are split and labelled
with the colour of `z`, such that each unsplit set through `z` has an uncoloured point in one of
them. The labels keep the guards of the two colours apart: the points coloured in order to settle a
point of colour `c` lie in finitely many sets labelled `c`, so each set labelled `!c` contains only
finitely many of them.

* Lemma 1 (`exists_hitting`): for nonempty finite sets there is a set `J` that meets all of them and
  each point of which is the only point of `J` in one of them (greedy choice along a well-ordering).
  Such a set `J` is a minimal hitting set.
* Lemma 2 (`guard_of_lone`): if `A k` is split and has label `c`, and `w` is its only point of
  colour `c`, then `{k}` is a guard of `w`. This is the main use of "no singleton intersections";
  the only other one is the choice of two points of `A a ∩ A b` in Lemma 6.
* Lemma 3 (`exists_settlingSet`): let `y` have colour `c`. Lemma 1, applied to the uncoloured points
  of the guard of `y` in the unsplit sets through `y`, gives the points `J` to be coloured next.
* Lemma 4 (`exists_settle`): give `J` the colour `!c`, and the label `!c` to the sets that become
  split. Then `y` is settled, the points of `J` are guarded by Lemma 2, and the other points keep
  their guards up to finitely many new members.
* Lemma 5 (`exists_closed`): settle one coloured point after the other.
* Lemma 6 (`exists_start`): how to begin with an unsplit set `A a`.

## Priority

New as far as known: no proof of the unrestricted statement, informal or formal, and no published
theorem that implies it was found in two independent searches on 24-25 Nov 2026. These searches were
limited and are not a certificate of novelty. Papers citing the source, Komjáth's related papers and
the splitting papers below (OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar) show no follow-up on this question. Not
seen, hence not excluded: the problem page and thread on the Erdős problems site (not requested);
arXiv (API not reachable); the full texts of Komjáth's 1989 paper (abstract read), his 1983 thesis
and his Erdős Centennial chapter; the Komjáth-Totik book; Zeraoulia's note; private repositories and
unread GitHub files (the code search gave 121 hits for "Erdos602" and 96 for "erdos_602", most of
them judged by path only). https://github.com/teorth/erdosproblems (25 Nov 2026) lists 602 as open,
with one post and one proof claim. Public mirrors (plasma-ai/erdos, which lists the claim as
rejected; przchojecki/agentic-erdos) indicate that this is Gao's claim on finite hypergraphs. What
appears to be new is the combination: closing off in countably many steps under a local invariant
that uses the no-singleton condition and needs no global minimal hitting set, together with its
formalization. The proof is short and elementary, and every single ingredient is known.

## Source and known cases

The question is P. Komjáth's, published by P. Erdős, "Some problems on finite and infinite graphs",
in: Logic and Combinatorics, Contemp. Math. 65 (1987) 223-228, item 12, p. 227,
https://www.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1987-28.pdf (doi:10.1090/conm/065/891250; page read): "Let
|A_i| = aleph_0, |A_i ∩ A_j| < aleph_0 and |A_i ∩ A_j| ≠ 1. Is such a family necessarily
two-chromatic?" The same item records that Komjáth "easily showed" that `ℵ₀` colours are enough when
only `|A_i ∩ A_j| ≠ 1` is assumed.

* Finite families: L. Lovász, Combinatorial Problems and Exercises, Problem 13.33.
* Splitting theorems (a partition `X₁ ∪ X₂` with `X₁` meeting each `A ∈ 𝓐` and `X₂` each `B ∈ 𝓑`,
  when `|A ∩ B| ≠ 1`): R. Ahlswede and L. H. Khachatrian, "Splitting properties in partially ordered
  sets and set systems" (preprint 1994), in: Numbers, Information and Complexity, Kluwer 2000,
  29-44, doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6048-4_4 (Thm 3: countable families; Thm 4: a family with a minimal
  hitting set; Cor 3: a well-ordering giving every member a maximum, or bounded degree, via J.
  Klimó, Discrete Appl. Math. 45 (1993) 161-168; Remark 7: finite members); Péter L. Erdős, Discrete
  Math. 163 (1997) 251-256, doi:10.1016/s0012-365x(96)00010-6, Cor 4, and Ann. Combin. 3 (1999)
  53-59, doi:10.1007/bf01609875; Péter L. Erdős and L. Soukup, Combinatorica 27 (2007) 147-161,
  doi:10.1007/s00493-007-0048-z, Lemma 2.6.
* Uniformly bounded intersections: E. W. Miller, C. R. Soc. Sci. Lett. Varsovie 30 (1937) 31-38; P.
  Erdős and A. Hajnal, Acta Math. Acad. Sci. Hungar. 12 (1961) 87-123, doi:10.1007/BF02066676; P.
  Komjáth, Acta Math. Hungar. 43 (1984) 199-207, doi:10.1007/BF01958019 (these three cited
  second-hand); a more general criterion: answer by bof, https://mathoverflow.net/questions/458059.
* Minimal hitting sets: P. Komjáth, Discrete Math. 342 (2019) 1849-1856,
  doi:10.1016/j.disc.2019.03.007 (review read); T. Csernák and L. Soukup,
  https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.10340. For countable edges they need uniformly bounded intersections.
* P. Komjáth, "Set systems with finite chromatic number", European J. Combin. 10 (1989) 543-549,
  doi:10.1016/S0195-6698(89)80071-X. Only the abstract was read. Nothing in the abstract claims the
  present theorem, but it mentions a Corollary 1 in Section 3 without stating it.

So countable families, families in which each point lies in at most countably many members, families
with a minimal hitting set or with a well-ordering as above, and families with uniformly bounded
intersections were known. What these results leave open lies among the uncountable families with
finite but unbounded intersections in which, for each finite `n`, some `n`-point set is contained in
uncountably many members. The criteria can fail there: for some families that satisfy the hypotheses
no well-ordering works (diagonalize an almost disjoint family of size continuum against all
well-orderings of a countable set; argument by an AI reviewer, not formalized), and under CH some
have no minimal hitting set (https://github.com/grandchallenge/MATHSOLVE/pull/1299, paper proof not
checked here). The latter does not affect the present proof, which takes minimal hitting sets only
of families of finite sets.

No printed statement contradicting the theorem was found. The nearest negative examples:

* The abstract of Komjáth 1989 announces, consistently, a system of `ℵ₁` countable sets without
  Property B, and states no condition on the intersections. If these sets are infinite, the present
  theorem forces two of them to meet in exactly one point or in infinitely many. The full text was
  not seen, so the system itself was not checked.
* https://mathoverflow.net/questions/275190 (D. van der Zypen) and Ahlswede-Khachatrian, Example 2:
  there the intersections are infinite.
* Miller's 1937 family does not exclude singleton intersections. It shows that "≠ 1" cannot be
  dropped.
* In the neighbouring problem 603 (`|A_i ∩ A_j| ≠ 2`), listed by public trackers as solved,
  singleton intersections are allowed.

